section: compiler
synopsis: ``noinline`` now works with specialisation and demand analysis
issues: #18396, #23911, 16588, #27724
mrs: !16658
description: {
  Saying ``(noinline f x)`` previously meant that the call would no longer be
  type-class specialised, nor would it be recognised as strict, nor would RULEs
  fire.  Now all of these things work.

  Moreover you can also say ``noinline (f <arg>)`` if you prefer, with the argument
  inside the ``noinline`` call.  Then ``f`` will not be inlined, but inlining within
  ``<arg>>`` is unaffected.

  Moreover, previously there was a danger that if ``f`` was defined trivially as
  ``f = Just`` (say) then it would be inlined anyway, despite the ``noinline``.
  Now ``noinline`` is fully respected.
}
